WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.728
RLHF in three steps.

2
00:00:02.008 --> 00:00:15.064
Reinforcement learning from human feedback was developed by Christiano and colleagues (2017), who trained agents from human comparisons of short behavior clips instead of a hand-written reward.

3
00:00:15.344 --> 00:00:23.045
The 2022 InstructGPT paper by Ouyang and colleagues applied it to language models in three steps.

4
00:00:23.325 --> 00:00:25.544
Step one, supervised fine-tuning.

5
00:00:25.824 --> 00:00:32.011
Labelers write demonstrations of good responses to prompts, and the pretrained model is fine-tuned on them.

6
00:00:32.291 --> 00:00:33.827
This is week 3’s technique.

7
00:00:34.107 --> 00:00:35.813
Step two, a reward model.

8
00:00:36.093 --> 00:00:47.037
The supervised model generates several responses per prompt, labelers rank them, and a separate model is trained to predict a scalar score such that preferred responses score higher.

9
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:56.896
The standard statistical model behind this is Bradley-Terry: the probability that response A beats response B is the sigmoid of the difference in their scores.

10
00:00:57.176 --> 00:00:59.160
Step three, reinforcement learning.

11
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:10.768
The language model is optimized, with the PPO algorithm, to produce responses the reward model scores highly, with a penalty on the KL divergence from the supervised model.

12
00:01:11.048 --> 00:01:19.901
The penalty matters: without it the model drifts toward text that exploits quirks of the reward model rather than text people actually prefer.

13
00:01:20.181 --> 00:01:24.192
The result that made this famous: labelers preferred outputs from a 1.

14
00:01:24.472 --> 00:01:32.472
3-billion-parameter InstructGPT model over those from the 175-billion-parameter GPT-3.

15
00:01:32.752 --> 00:01:34.224
The cost is complexity.

16
00:01:34.504 --> 00:01:43.464
RLHF needs a reward model, samples from the policy during training, several models in memory at once, and careful tuning to stay stable.

17
00:01:43.744 --> 00:02:00.533
Anthropic’s Constitutional AI work (Bai and colleagues, 2022) showed one way to reduce the human labeling: generate the comparisons with a model guided by a written set of principles, an approach usually called RL from AI feedback.
